# The two screens: the server STAGE (view + card + controls) and a client VIEWPORT
# (view + card). Both draw the same card over a full-bleed 3dview, so the server tab
# alone is enough to walk the whole range, and extra browser tabs / consoles are only
# needed for the specimens that are about per-client differences.
#
# Repaint is by re-entering the label, never by updating a child in place: an in-place
# child update inside an absolutely-positioned region ghosts in the ENGINE (and the mock
# cannot reproduce it), which is exactly the class of bug this range exists to catch. It
# must not be the range's own bug.

@console/visual_range !0 ^1 "Visual Range"
" The specimen stage: the 3D view, the card, and Prev / Re-run / Next.
#
# A CONSOLE entry as well as the reroute, so a client that connects late - or one that gets
# sent back to console select - can still choose the stage. The reroute only catches clients
# that are already connected when the map starts.
    jump visual_stage


=== visual_stage
    # A scripted camera needs FOUR things in the engine, not two. Registering the widget list
    # is the one that was missing, and without it the console simply has no viewport - the
    # camera is streamed and lands nowhere. gui_layout_widget only PLACES a view the console
    # has already declared; it does not declare it.
    #   1. gui_activate_console  - the console has a type
    #   2. gui_update_widget_list - it declares a 3dview
    #   3. gui_layout_widget      - the 3dview is placed in the layout
    #   4. assignment + cinematic_control (below)
    # Console name copied from LM's Game Master, the proven scripted-camera console.
    gui_activate_console("gamemaster_sci")
    gui_update_widget_list("3dview")
    gui_layout_widget("3dview")
    # Camera setup runs as its own TASK, not inline, because the engine wants a beat between
    # the steps: LM's Game Master waits a full second between spawning its cambot and
    # assigning a client to it, and only sets the view up after that. Doing all three inside
    # one console build is a plausible cause of a camera that works only sometimes.
    if visual_camera_needs_setup(client_id):
        task_schedule(visual_client_camera_setup, {"cam_client": client_id})
        # ...and a SYNC loop, the way both working consoles do it. The Game Master re-applies
        # its camera on every selection change AND unconditionally every ~15s ("every 15 sec
        # move camra anyway"); Manual Beams re-applies on every console rebuild. Neither
        # relies on a single call landing. This range fired once per change, from a
        # SPECIMEN's task rather than from the console - so a call lost to a race was never
        # corrected, which is what "works sometimes" looks like from the outside.
        task_schedule(visual_camera_sync, {"cam_client": client_id})

    # AND apply it inline, here in the console's own build, exactly where Manual Beams does
    # (`gui_cinematic_full_control` sits in its build, before its await gui()). Every working
    # console sets its camera from a GUI context - Manual Beams inline, the Game Master from a
    # gui_sub_task of its console. This range only ever set it from plain tasks with no page
    # context, which is the last structural difference left between it and the two that work.
    #
    # It also makes the repaint the real heartbeat: the stage rebuilds on every card change,
    # so the camera is re-asserted whenever a specimen changes anything.
    #visual_camera_apply(client_id)

    # A specimen about PER-CLIENT differences (what this console can see vs another) has to
    # put this console on a ship - the FX stream is culled around the assigned ship, not the
    # camera. A specimen that cares tags one ship "visual_client_ship"; the rest never do,
    # and this is a no-op for them.
    _mine = to_object_list(role("visual_client_ship"))
    if len(_mine) > 0:
        sbs.assign_client_to_ship(client_id, _mine[0].id)
    await delay_sim(0)
    visual_camera_apply(client_id)

    # The card, bottom left, over the view.
    gui_section("area: 1, 52, 46, 99;")
    for _line in visual_card_lines():
        gui_row("row-height: content; padding: 2px, 4px, 2px, 6px;")
        gui_text(_line)

    # Controls a SPECIMEN asked for. Only a console can build GUI, so a specimen about how a
    # control LOOKS has nowhere else to put it; it declares them as data and they are drawn
    # here, over the live view - which is also the only place their draw order can be judged.
    _specs = visual_widget_specs()
    if len(_specs) > 0:
        gui_section("area: 50, 40, 99, 86;")
        for _ws in _specs:
            gui_row("row-height: 2.4em; padding: 3px, 6px, 3px, 6px;")
            _sb = gui_button(_ws[0])
            if _ws[1] != "":
                _sb.background_color = _ws[1]

    # Controls, bottom right. Named widgets, not inline calls: an inline
    # `on gui_message(gui_button("x")):` is accepted by the mock and rejected by the
    # engine's stricter parser.
    gui_section("area: 62, 88, 99, 99;")
    gui_row("row-height: 1fr;")
    _btn_prev = gui_button("< Prev")
    _btn_again = gui_button("Re-run")
    _btn_next = gui_button("Next >")
    gui_row("row-height: content;")
    gui_text(visual_text(visual_stage_position(), "gui-1", "#8fa4bd"))

    on gui_message(_btn_prev):
        visual_step(-1)
        task_schedule(visual_run_index)
    on gui_message(_btn_again):
        task_schedule(visual_run_index)
    on gui_message(_btn_next):
        visual_step(1)
        task_schedule(visual_run_index)

    # A specimen finishing bumps the card sequence; repaint so its card is the one on screen.
    on change visual_card_seq():
        jump visual_stage
    on change visual_camera_seq():
        jump visual_stage

    await gui()


=== visual_viewport
    gui_activate_console("gamemaster_sci")
    gui_update_widget_list("3dview")
    gui_layout_widget("3dview")
    if visual_camera_needs_setup(client_id):
        task_schedule(visual_client_camera_setup, {"cam_client": client_id})

    gui_section("area: 1, 52, 46, 99;")
    for _line in visual_card_lines():
        gui_row("row-height: content; padding: 2px, 4px, 2px, 6px;")
        gui_text(_line)

    gui_section("area: 62, 94, 99, 99;")
    gui_row("row-height: 1fr;")
    gui_text(visual_text("viewport - drive the range from the server tab", "gui-1", "#6f8296"))

    on change visual_card_seq():
        jump visual_viewport
    on change visual_camera_seq():
        jump visual_viewport

    await gui()


=== visual_client_camera_setup
metadata: ``` yaml
cam_client: 0
```
    # Spawn, wait, assign, wait, point. The waits are the point: the Game Master sequences it
    # this way and the range did not. Loops so it self-heals - every specimen switch deletes
    # the cambot along with the rest of the scene, and the console must get a new one without
    # anybody remembering to ask.
---cam_setup
    _cam = visual_client_cambot(cam_client)
    ->END if _cam is None
    await delay_sim(1)
    visual_client_assign_one(cam_client, _cam)
    await delay_sim(1)
    visual_camera_apply(cam_client)
---cam_watch
    await delay_sim(2)
    jump cam_setup if not object_exists(_cam)
    jump cam_watch


=== visual_camera_sync
metadata: ``` yaml
cam_client: 0
```
    # Re-apply this console's current pin, forever. Cheap - it is one engine call every couple
    # of seconds - and it means a dropped camera heals itself instead of needing a specimen to
    # change something before the view comes back.
---cam_beat
    await delay_sim(2)
    visual_camera_apply(cam_client)
    jump cam_beat
